<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each network-policy pair, we report the full-precision accuracy of the network and the change in accuracy after quantization in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Obtaining Robust and Detrimental Policies ‣ 4 EXPERIMENTS ‣ Assessing the Potential for Catastrophic Failurein Dynamic Post-Training Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. For the robust <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{R},\pi_{R}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{R},\pi_{R}\}</annotation></semantics></math> pairs, we see that for many of the budgets and bit-width options, we can obtain pairs with full-precision performance near the original white box model (which was the initialization for the <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> network) that produce a small <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;2\%</annotation></semantics></math> decrease in accuracy once quantized. It is a much more difficult task to find a detrimental <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{D},\pi_{D}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{D},\pi_{D}\}</annotation></semantics></math> pair (where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> performs similar to <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>), but we are still able to find some notable pairs, which are highlighted in purple in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Obtaining Robust and Detrimental Policies ‣ 4 EXPERIMENTS ‣ Assessing the Potential for Catastrophic Failurein Dynamic Post-Training Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Most notably is the pair with ResNet18 as the network, which obtained full-precision performance which was better than the original white box model and within <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1\%</annotation></semantics></math> of its respective full-precision <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> counterpart. While this particular <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> model performed well, and was essentially indistinguishable from its <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> counterpart at full-precision, it incurred a <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{-}24.39\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>24.39</mn></mrow><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{-}24.39\%</annotation></semantics></math> reduction in accuracy once quantized, compared to a <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{-}0.22\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0.22</mn></mrow><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{-}0.22\%</annotation></semantics></math> reduction for the <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{R},\pi_{R}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{R},\pi_{R}\}</annotation></semantics></math> pair with the exact same budget. Thus, we were able to find a detrimental network-policy pair that demonstrated the existence of catastrophic failure stemming from DPTQ.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For any two well-performing networks trained on the same dataset (<em id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{D}</annotation></semantics></math>), there is going to exist some subset of test examples that both models classify correctly. However, if quantization results in one model encountering a small reduction in performance and the other model having a much more drastic degradation in performance, then the aforementioned subset of test examples is obviously going to decrease. We define the points that <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> originally both get correct, but get classified incorrectly by <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f_{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f_{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> (the quantized version of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{D}</annotation></semantics></math>) as “transitory points”. Such inputs are considered “at risk” since they are more susceptible to incorrect prediction due to quantization. Being able to identify these transitory points is equally as important to safety-critical deep learning as identifying the existence of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{D},\pi_{D}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{D},\pi_{D}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, since these sorts of examples are going to be the source for catastrophic failure when considering DPTQ in practice. For each of the groups of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{R},\pi_{R}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{R},\pi_{R}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{D},\pi_{D}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{D},\pi_{D}\}</annotation></semantics></math> pairs highlighted in purple in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Obtaining Robust and Detrimental Policies ‣ 4 EXPERIMENTS ‣ Assessing the Potential for Catastrophic Failurein Dynamic Post-Training Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we show the percentage of transitory points between the pairs in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1 Obtaining Robust and Detrimental Policies ‣ 4 EXPERIMENTS ‣ Assessing the Potential for Catastrophic Failurein Dynamic Post-Training Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. We present this as an initial exploration into transitory points, with further investigation left as future work.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Test accuracy for all network-policy pairs in <math id="S4.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{N},f_{R},f_{D}\}\times\{\pi_{R},\pi_{D},\text{random quantization}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.430em" minsize="1.430em">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.430em" minsize="1.430em" rspace="0.055em">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.430em" minsize="1.430em">{</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext>random quantization</mtext><mo maxsize="1.430em" minsize="1.430em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{N},f_{R},f_{D}\}\times\{\pi_{R},\pi_{D},\text{random quantization}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T3.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
