<td id="S4.F2.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_align_center" style="padding:2.5pt 0.0pt;" colspan="3">Relative Layer Depth (Input Layer <math id="S4.F2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> Pre-Classifier Layer)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Train and test accuracy of the highlight models from Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Obtaining Robust and Detrimental Policies ‣ 4 EXPERIMENTS ‣ Assessing the Potential for Catastrophic Failurein Dynamic Post-Training Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> when certain layers are quantized and others are left at full-precision. “Before” quantizes all layers <span id="S4.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">before</span> that point (the relative layer on the x-axis), “after” quantizes all layers <span id="S4.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">after</span> that point, and “single” quantizes just that single layer. All other layer activations remain at full-precision.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Knowing there could exist some layers whose activations are more susceptible to quantization error than others, we explored this further by examining only quantizing certain subsets of layer activations and leaving others at full-precision. We investigated three different schemes for isolated quantization: “before”, “after”, and “single”. Given some layer <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in[1,L]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in[1,L]</annotation></semantics></math>, “before” will quantize all layers <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">before</span> <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> with everything else being full-precision. Conversely, “after” will quantize all layers <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">after</span> <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> with the previous layers left at full-precision (<em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em>, the inverse of “before”). Finally, “single” will quantize the activations output from just that individual layer and all other layers remain at full-precision. These different variations allows us to isolate the effects of quantization on specific layers and their impact and cascading effects on the overall network. Once a network is quantized using any of the aforementioned configurations, we compute the accuracy on the train and test sets, which should allow us to see where degradation begins to occur in a model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We plot the scores for all dynamically quantizable layer activations in the highlighted ResNet18, MobileNetV4-Conv-Medium, and RegNetX-1.6GF models and present these results in Fig. <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2 Network Layer Analysis ‣ 4 EXPERIMENTS ‣ Assessing the Potential for Catastrophic Failurein Dynamic Post-Training Quantization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Each of the models appear to have different points at which the quantization ultimately leads to catastrophic failure. For ResNet18, this occurs about <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="75\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>75</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">75\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the way through the network and in roughly the last <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the RegNetX-1.6GF model. Coincidentally, this happens to be the last 6-10 layers for both networks. As for the MobileNetV4-Conv-Medium network, we interestingly observe much different behavior where the degradation stems from the earlier layers of the model. We note that the MobileNetV4 family of models is specifically designed for mobile/edge deployment compared to ResNet and RegNet which were developed for more general usage. Thus, there could be architectural differences and certain design choices that lead to this difference in behaviors. We leave further investigation into these differences as future work. Looking at the “single” plots, we see that if we quantize just the layers that are inflection points in “before” and “after”, these layers alone are enough to cause significant performance reduction. Thus, one could consider leaving these crucial layers at full-precision and only quantizing what remains.</p>
</div>
